{"file":{"content":"The next target is now correctly isolated:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{prove high-conductor damping by oscillatory cancellation, not by positivity, not by bounded descent.}\n}\n]\n\nThe immediate task is to turn\n\n[\n|M_\\chi^n|^2\n]\n\ninto explicit (p)-adic character sums, identify the rational functions\n\n[\nR_{w,w'}(a)=\\frac{\\Phi_w(a)}{\\Phi_{w'}(a)},\n]\n\nand classify when they are stationary/coboundary-like.\n\nHere is the concrete plan I would use.\n\n## 1. Start with (n=2), not (n=3)\n\nFor a branch word\n\n[\nw=(b_0,b_1),\n]\n\nwrite the residue evolution as\n\n[\nS_b(a)=(3a+1)2^{-b}.\n]\n\nThen\n\n[\nS_{b_1}S_{b_0}(a)\n=================\n\n# \\left(3(3a+1)2^{-b_0}+1\\right)2^{-b_1}\n\n(9a+3+2^{b_0})2^{-(b_0+b_1)}.\n]\n\nThe character phase for a two-step branch should therefore involve affine factors of the form\n\n[\n\\Phi_{b_0,b_1}(a)\n=================\n\n9a+3+2^{b_0},\n]\n\nup to powers of (2), which are harmless but character-visible.\n\nThus the basic pair-correlation rational functions are\n\n[\nR_{w,w'}(a)\n===========\n\n\\frac{9a+3+2^{b_0}}{9a+3+2^{b'_0}}\n\\cdot\n2^{-(b_0+b_1)+(b'_0+b'_1)}\n]\n\nor the corresponding formula with parity/internal indices included.\n\nThe first audit should extract exactly these (R_{w,w'}), reduce them modulo (3^c), and group them by whether\n\n[\nR_{w,w'}(a)\n]\n\nis constant, a square/coboundary-like term, or genuinely varying.\n\n## 2. Stationary phase test\n\nFor a primitive character (\\chi) modulo (3^c), sums of the form\n\n[\nC_c(R,\\chi)\n===========\n\n\\frac{1}{\\varphi(3^c)}\n\\sum_{a\\in U_{3^c}}\\chi(R(a))\n]\n\nare large only if (R) is nearly stationary in the (3)-adic sense.\n\nFor\n\n[\nR(a)=\\frac{\\alpha a+\\beta}{\\alpha' a+\\beta'},\n]\n\nthe logarithmic derivative is\n\n[\n\\frac{R'(a)}{R(a)}\n==================\n\n## \\frac{\\alpha}{\\alpha a+\\beta}\n\n\\frac{\\alpha'}{\\alpha'a+\\beta'}.\n]\n\nFor the two-step functions above,\n\n[\n\\alpha=\\alpha'=9,\n]\n\nso\n\n[\n\\frac{R'(a)}{R(a)}\n==================\n\n9\\left(\n\\frac{1}{9a+3+2^{b_0}}\n----------------------\n\n\\frac{1}{9a+3+2^{b'_0}}\n\\right).\n]\n\nThis vanishes identically only when\n\n[\n2^{b_0}\\equiv2^{b'_0}\\pmod{3^c}.\n]\n\nBecause (2) is primitive modulo (3^c), that means\n\n[\nb_0\\equiv b'_0\\pmod{\\varphi(3^c)}.\n]\n\nFor bounded branches, this usually means\n\n[\nb_0=b'_0.\n]\n\nSo the expected classification is:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\tb_0=b'*0\n\t\\Rightarrow\n\tR*{w,w'}\\text{ essentially constant/coboundary-like;}\n}\n]\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\tb_0\\ne b'*0\n\t\\Rightarrow\n\tR*{w,w'}\\text{ has nonzero logarithmic derivative and should cancel.}\n}\n]\n\nThat is the first rigorous-looking stationary/nonstationary split.\n\n## 3. What to compute for (n=2)\n\nFor each pair\n\n[\nw=(b_0,b_1),\\qquad w'=(b'_0,b'_1)\n]\n\nwith\n\n[\nb_i,b'_i\\le B,\n]\n\ncompute:\n\n[\nR_{w,w'}(a)\n===========\n\n\\frac{\\Phi_w(a)}{\\Phi_{w'}(a)}.\n]\n\nThen compute the invariant\n\n[\n\\Delta_{w,w'}(a)\n================\n\n\\frac{R'*{w,w'}(a)}{R*{w,w'}(a)}.\n]\n\nRecord:\n\n[\nv_3(\\Delta_{w,w'}(a))\n]\n\nas (a) ranges over (U_{3^c}).\n\nClassify each pair into:\n\n[\n\\text{constant},\n]\n\n[\n\\text{coboundary/quadratic},\n]\n\n[\n\\text{nonstationary with derivative valuation }d.\n]\n\nThen compute exact sums\n\n[\nC_{w,w'}(c,k)\n=============\n\n\\frac1{\\varphi(3^c)}\n\\sum_{a\\in U_{3^c}}\n\\chi_k(R_{w,w'}(a)).\n]\n\nThe key table should be:\n\n[\n\\begin{array}{c|c|c|c}\n\t(w,w') & \\min_a v_3(R'/R) & \\max_k |C_{w,w'}(c,k)| & \\text{class}\\\n\t\\hline\n\\end{array}\n]\n\nYou are looking for:\n\n[\n|C_{w,w'}(c,k)|\\le 1-\\delta\n]\n\nfor every non-coboundary/nonconstant pair, preferably with decay as (c) grows.\n\n## 4. Then do (n=3)\n\nFor\n\n[\nw=(b_0,b_1,b_2),\n]\n\nthe affine numerator is\n\n[\n\\Phi_w(a)\n=========\n\n27a+9+3\\cdot2^{b_0}+2^{b_0+b_1}.\n]\n\nIn general,\n\n[\nS_w(a)\n======\n\n\\left(\n3^n a+\n\\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}3^{n-1-j}2^{B_j}\n\\right)2^{-B_n},\n]\n\nwhere\n\n[\nB_j=b_0+\\cdots+b_{j-1},\n\\qquad B_0=0.\n]\n\nSo define\n\n[\nA_w(a)\n======\n\n3^n a+\n\\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}3^{n-1-j}2^{B_j}.\n]\n\nThen\n\n[\nR_{w,w'}(a)\n===========\n\n\\frac{A_w(a)}{A_{w'}(a)}\n\\cdot\n2^{-B_n+B'_n}.\n]\n\nThis is the universal formula. It should become the central definition in §7C.\n\nThe logarithmic derivative is\n\n[\n\\frac{R'*{w,w'}(a)}{R*{w,w'}(a)}\n================================\n\n3^n\\left(\\frac{1}{A_w(a)}-\\frac{1}{A_{w'}(a)}\\right).\n]\n\nThus stationarity is controlled by\n\n[\nA_w(a)-A_{w'}(a).\n]\n\nSince both have the same linear coefficient (3^n a), the difference is constant in (a):\n\n[\nA_w(a)-A_{w'}(a)\n================\n\nC_w-C_{w'}.\n]\n\nTherefore either:\n\n[\nC_w=C_{w'}\n]\n\nand the ratio is constant, or\n\n[\nC_w\\ne C_{w'}\n]\n\nand the derivative is nonzero with valuation determined by\n\n[\nv_3(C_w-C_{w'}).\n]\n\nThis is excellent. The stationary classification becomes a finite word-collision problem:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\tC_w=C_{w'}\\pmod{3^c}\n}\n]\n\nwhere\n\n[\nC_w=\n\\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}3^{n-1-j}2^{B_j}.\n]\n\nThat is now fully explicit.\n\n## 5. The key lemma to aim for\n\nThe (n)-step character sums reduce to rational functions\n\n[\nR_{w,w'}(a)=\n\\frac{3^n a+C_w}{3^n a+C_{w'}}\n\\cdot 2^{-B_n+B'_n}.\n]\n\nThe derivative criterion is:\n\n[\n\\frac{R'}{R}\n============\n\n3^n\n\\frac{C_{w'}-C_w}\n{(3^n a+C_w)(3^n a+C_{w'})}.\n]\n\nSince the denominators are units for admissible (a), the derivative valuation is\n\n[\nv_3(R'/R)\n=========\n\nn+v_3(C_w-C_{w'}).\n]\n\nSo if\n\n[\nv_3(C_w-C_{w'})\\le c-n-C_0,\n]\n\nthen the phase is genuinely oscillatory modulo (3^c), and primitive-character sums should cancel.\n\nThe dangerous pairs are exactly those with\n\n[\nC_w\\equiv C_{w'}\\pmod{3^{c-n-O(1)}}.\n]\n\nThose are near-collisions of word constants.\n\nThus the live analytic problem becomes:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{control near-collisions of }C_w\n\t=====================================\n\t\n\t\\sum_{j<n}3^{n-1-j}2^{B_j}.\n}\n]\n\nThis is very concrete.\n\n## 6. Van der Corput bound shape\n\nA primitive character (\\chi) modulo (3^c) composed with\n\n[\nR(a)=\\frac{3^n a+C}{3^n a+C'}\n]\n\nshould satisfy a bound of the form:\n\n[\n\\left|\n\\sum_{a\\in U_{3^c}}\\chi(R(a))\n\\right|\n\\le\n3^{c-\\alpha}\n]\n\nwhenever\n\n[\nv_3(C-C')\\le c-n-\\alpha.\n]\n\nEven a weak bound with fixed (\\alpha) is enough if enough word-pair mass is nonstationary.\n\nYou do not need square-root cancellation. You need to show that the total pair mass of stationary/near-stationary pairs is bounded away from (1).\n\nSo the proof splits into:\n\n1. **Stationary pair classification:** pairs with (C_w\\equiv C_{w'}) to high (3)-adic order are rare unless they correspond to the coboundary sector.\n2. **Nonstationary cancellation:** all other pairs have bounded cancellation by (p)-adic stationary phase.\n\nThis is now a strong route.\n\n## 7. Concrete numerical outputs to generate\n\nFor (n=2,3,4), and branch cutoff (B), compute weighted pair mass by derivative valuation:\n\n[\nP_{n,c}(r)\n==========\n\n\\sum_{w,w':\\ v_3(C_w-C_{w'})=r}\np(w)p(w').\n]\n\nAlso compute cumulative:\n\n[\nP_{n,c}^{\\ge R}\n===============\n\n\\sum_{r\\ge R}P_{n,c}(r).\n]\n\nYou want to see that for some (n), most mass has\n\n[\nr\n]\n\nsmall, hence oscillatory.\n\nThen compute exact character sums grouped by (r):\n\n[\nS_r(c)\n======\n\n\\max_{\\substack{w,w':\\v_3(C_w-C_{w'})=r}}\n\\max_{\\chi\\ primitive}\n\\left|\n\\frac1{\\varphi(3^c)}\n\\sum_a\\chi(R_{w,w'}(a))\n\\right|.\n]\n\nThe desired empirical pattern:\n\n[\nS_r(c)\\ll 3^{-(c-n-r)/2}\n]\n\nor at least\n\n[\nS_r(c)\\le 1-\\delta\n]\n\nwhen\n\n[\nc-n-r\\ge C_0.\n]\n\nFinally estimate the total (L^2) contraction:\n\n[\n\\sum_r P_{n,c}(r)S_r(c).\n]\n\nIf this is\n\n[\n<1-\\delta\n]\n\nfor some fixed (n) or (n=O(c)), you have the empirical proof skeleton.\n\n## 8. What formula to put in the paper\n\nAdd a proposition:\n\n[\n\\textbf{Pair-correlation phase formula.}\n]\n\nFor a word (w=(b_0,\\dots,b_{n-1})), set\n\n[\nB_j(w)=\\sum_{i<j}b_i,\n]\n\n[\nC_w=\\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}3^{n-1-j}2^{B_j(w)}.\n]\n\nThen\n\n[\nS_w(a)\n======\n\n(3^n a+C_w)2^{-B_n(w)}.\n]\n\nFor two words (w,w'),\n\n[\nR_{w,w'}(a)=\n\\frac{S_w(a)}{S_{w'}(a)}\n========================\n\n2^{-B_n(w)+B_n(w')}\n\\frac{3^n a+C_w}{3^n a+C_{w'}}.\n]\n\nMoreover,\n\n[\n\\frac{R'*{w,w'}(a)}{R*{w,w'}(a)}\n================================\n\n3^n\n\\frac{C_{w'}-C_w}\n{(3^n a+C_w)(3^n a+C_{w'})}.\n]\n\nThus stationarity is equivalent to high (3)-adic divisibility of\n\n[\nC_w-C_{w'}.\n]\n\nThis proposition is the analytic engine.\n\n## 9. What to try proving first\n\nBefore proving full high-conductor damping, prove the following modest lemma.\n\n[\n\\textbf{Nonstationary pair cancellation lemma.}\n]\n\nLet (\\chi) be primitive modulo (3^c). Suppose\n\n[\nv_3(C_w-C_{w'})\\le c-n-2.\n]\n\nThen\n\n[\n\\left|\n\\frac1{\\varphi(3^c)}\n\\sum_{a\\in U_{3^c}}\n\\chi(R_{w,w'}(a))\n\\right|\n\\le \\theta\n]\n\nfor some absolute\n\n[\n\\theta<1.\n]\n\nThis is much weaker than square-root cancellation, but it may be enough.\n\nThen prove a combinatorial lemma:\n\n[\n\\textbf{Nonstationary mass lemma.}\n]\n\nFor some fixed or slowly growing (n), the weighted mass of pairs satisfying\n\n[\nv_3(C_w-C_{w'})\\le c-n-2\n]\n\nis at least\n\n[\n\\alpha>0.\n]\n\nThen the (L^2) norm contracts by at least\n\n[\n\\alpha(1-\\theta).\n]\n\nThis gives high-conductor damping.\n\n## 10. Bottom line\n\nThe next computation should not be a black-box spectral computation. It should output the pair-correlation arithmetic:\n\n[\nC_w,\\quad C_{w'},\\quad v_3(C_w-C_{w'}),\\quad\nR_{w,w'}(a),\\quad\n\\sum_a\\chi(R_{w,w'}(a)).\n]\n\nThe core formula is:\n\n[\nR_{w,w'}(a)\n===========\n\n2^{-B_n+B'*n}\n\\frac{3^n a+C_w}{3^n a+C*{w'}}.\n]\n\nAnd the core proof target is:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{nonstationary word pairs have }p\\text{-adic character cancellation,}\n}\n]\n\nwhile near-stationary pairs are either low-mass or coboundary-classified.\n\nThat is now the cleanest route to the high-conductor damping conjecture.\n","filePath":"/home/john/mynotes/deterministic_equidistribution_theory/critiques/dc_13.md","numLines":687,"startLine":1,"totalLines":687},"type":"text"}